    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d335-a1">

                <pb facs="0627" n="557" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg557"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Mosby to
                        Collector Sullivan.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Hong-Kong</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-03-07"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 7,
                        1883</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The English steamer Glenelg cleared from
                    this port for Honolulu a few days ago with 800 tons of sugar. I think it
                    probable that an attempt will be made to-enter this sugar as Honolulu sugar,
                    free of duty, at San Francisco. I learn, also, that the steamship Madras and
                    steamship C. T. Hook will leave soon for Honolulu, loaded with sugar. I think
                    the United States consul there should be advised of these facts, in order that
                    he may detect any attempt to smuggle this sugar into the United States duty
                    free.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN S. MOSBY,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States
                            Consul.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
